  {
   "tipo": "explicacion",
   "titulo": "Norwegian letters",
   "texto": [
    "The Norwegian alphabet uses the latina letters and adds the following, and the end of it. \"It looks like an open «e»;\" ø does not have an exact equivalent in Spanish and it is produced by rounding the lips; \"\" å sounds like a closed «or».",
    "In Norwegian, the names are usually written the same even if the pronunciation is not always degenerated letter by letter. To order the spelling: Kan du stave navnet ditt? («Can you spell your name?»).",
    "Kan is followed by the infinitive without å: kan stave, no kan å stave. Navn is neutral; its defined form is navnet («the name»), with the article added at the end of the noun."
   ]
  },
